.TH SECSTORETEST 1
.SH NAME
secstoretest \- test the secstore network round-trip
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B secstoretest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Secstoretest
is a self-contained regression test for the
.IR secstore (2)
client/server exchange.
It stands up a minimal secstore server on a loopback socket \(em using the
library's own
.B pakserver
plus a small in-memory
.BR GET / PUT
loop that speaks the same wire protocol as
.B secstore->getfile
and
.B secstore->putfile
\(em then, as a client, runs the whole library path: dial, push
.IR ssl (3),
authenticate with PAK,
.B putfile
an encrypted blob,
.B getfile
it back, and check it is byte-identical.
.PP
It then runs a negative control: a second client using the wrong password
must fail to authenticate.
A pass therefore means the PAK verifier is actually being checked rather than
waved through, and that a correct exchange survives the switch to the encrypted
channel.
.PP
It binds
.B #I
onto
.B /net
itself and needs no network, account, or password setup.
It prints a line per check and a final
.B PASS
or
.BR FAIL ,
and exits with an exception on failure.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/secstoretest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR secstore (1),
.IR secstore (2),
.IR secstored (8),
.IR factotum (4)
.SH BUGS
The server side is a stripped-down stand-in, not
.IR secstored (8)
itself, so account creation, the file listing format, STA/SecurID, and the
.B RM
verb are not exercised here \(em only the PAK authentication and a single
file's encrypted round trip, which is where the network-path bugs lived.
It uses a fixed loopback port, so two copies cannot run at once.
